package wasdk:media@2.3.0;

/// Images decoded and encoded by the system's codecs, as Apple's Image I/O, Android's
/// `ImageDecoder` and `Bitmap.compress`, Windows' `BitmapDecoder` and `BitmapEncoder` and the web's
/// `ImageDecoder` do it, so a program reads and writes every format the system does without a codec
/// of its own.
///
/// **Two kinds of memory, and every move a call.** An image decodes into the program's memory, a
/// `pixel-buffer`, on every system (`decode-pixels`), which the program uploads with `wasdk:gpu` itself
/// to draw, and encodes from one (`encode-pixels`). Where the system can, its hardware codecs
/// (`hardware-image-codecs`) decode into GPU memory, a `hardware-buffer` of the program's own, which
/// it draws through a texture it imports with no copy (`wasdk:platform/gpu-interop`) and which
/// holds no slot in a pool, and encode from one, a buffer the program allocated and drew or a video
/// frame's among them: Apple's codecs decode into an `IOSurface`, and Android's `ImageDecoder`
/// allocates a hardware bitmap. A hardware buffer comes
/// back to the program's memory only through its `read-pixels`, so no pixels move that the program
/// did not ask to move.
///
/// **Opening.** An image is opened, as an `image-source`, from its encoded bytes (`open`), or from
/// a file of the program's (`open-file`), which the system reads itself, only as far as it needs,
/// as a photo's metadata without its pixels, so a large file need not cross into the program.
/// Opening reads what the image says of itself, its format, size, pictures, orientation and
/// metadata; its pixels are decoded when asked, at the size asked.
///
/// **Formats.** A format is its MIME type, such as `image/jpeg`, `image/png` or `image/heic`. The
/// codecs list those they decode and those they encode, which differ between systems and between
/// machines.
///
/// **Metadata and location.** An image's metadata is what its file says, EXIF's common fields, a
/// field none where the file says nothing. A photo's location is the user's location data, given as
/// the file holds it to the program that holds the file, which could read it from the bytes itself,
/// so reading it asks nothing; where a system takes it out of the files a program reads, as
/// Android does without its media location permission, it is none. Encoding writes no metadata, so
/// pixels encoded again carry no location.
///
/// **Mistakes.** A picture the image does not have, pixels whose bytes are not `height` rows of
/// `row-bytes` or whose rows are shorter than four bytes a pixel, a quality outside 0.0 to 1.0 and
/// a `max-size` under a pixel across are the program's mistakes, and trap.
interface image-codecs {
  use wasi:clocks/types@0.3.0.{duration};
  use wasdk:platform/windows@2.3.0.{size};
  use wasdk:platform/hardware-buffers@2.3.0.{hardware-buffer, pixel-buffer, image-error};

  /// The system's image codecs; none where it has none.
  get-image-codecs: func() -> option<image-codecs>;

  resource image-codecs {
    /// The formats it decodes, by MIME type.
    decoded-formats: func() -> list<string>;
    /// The formats it encodes, by MIME type.
    encoded-formats: func() -> list<string>;
    /// The image `data` encodes, in one of the `decoded-formats`, read as far as its size,
    /// pictures, orientation and metadata. `unsupported` for bytes of no format it decodes,
    /// `damaged` for bytes it cannot read that far.
    open: async func(data: list<u8>) -> result<image-source, image-error>;
    /// The image in the file at `path`, an absolute path the program can read, which the system
    /// reads only as far as it needs. `not-found` for a path with no file among the program's.
    open-file: async func(path: string) -> result<image-source, image-error>;
    /// `pixels` encoded in `format`, one of the `encoded-formats`, at `quality` from 0.0, the
    /// smallest, to 1.0, the best, where the format loses detail, or the system's own where none;
    /// a lossless format keeps every pixel whatever the quality. `unsupported` for a format it
    /// does not encode.
    encode-pixels: async func(pixels: pixel-buffer, format: string, quality: option<f32>) -> result<list<u8>, image-error>;
    /// The system's hardware codecs; none where it neither decodes into hardware buffers nor
    /// encodes from them.
    hardware-image-codecs: func() -> option<hardware-image-codecs>;
  }

  /// An image opened by the system's codecs from its encoded bytes, as Apple's `CGImageSource` and
  /// Android's `ImageDecoder.Source` are: what it says of itself, read as it opened, and its
  /// pictures decoded as they are asked for.
  resource image-source {
    /// Its format's MIME type, one of the `decoded-formats`.
    mime-type: func() -> string;
    /// Its size in pixels, upright, as its primary picture decodes at full size: a still image's
    /// one, an animation's frames, which all share it, or a document's primary page.
    size: func() -> size;
    /// How its pictures go: one still picture, the frames of an animation, or the pages of a
    /// document.
    pictures: func() -> image-pictures;
    /// How the file stores its primary picture turned from upright. It is decoded upright, and
    /// `size` is upright, so a program reads it to show it or to keep it.
    orientation: func() -> image-orientation;
    /// What the file says of how its primary picture was taken.
    metadata: func() -> image-metadata;
    /// Picture `index` in the program's memory, upright, scaled down to fit `max-size`, in pixels,
    /// keeping its shape, where given; in a format the system picks, which the buffer names. The
    /// index is 0 for a still image, a frame's for an animation and a page's for a document.
    decode-pixels: async func(index: u32, max-size: option<size>) -> result<pixel-buffer, image-error>;
  }

  /// The system's codecs between encoded images and hardware buffers, with no copy through the
  /// program's memory.
  resource hardware-image-codecs {
    /// Picture `index` of `image`, as `decode-pixels` names it, in a hardware buffer of the
    /// program's own, for sampling, upright, scaled down to fit `max-size`, in pixels, keeping its
    /// shape, where given.
    /// `too-large` where it is larger than a hardware buffer the system makes, and `out-of-memory`
    /// where the system has no memory left for it, as an allocation answers.
    decode: async func(image: borrow<image-source>, index: u32, max-size: option<size>) -> result<hardware-buffer, image-error>;
    /// `buffer`'s pixels encoded in `format`, one of the image codecs' `encoded-formats`, at
    /// `quality` as `encode-pixels` takes it, once the GPU has finished the work the program
    /// submitted and the writes it queued before this call. `unsupported` for a format the system
    /// does not encode, or a buffer whose pixels it does not read.
    encode: async func(buffer: borrow<hardware-buffer>, format: string, quality: option<f32>) -> result<list<u8>, image-error>;
  }

  /// How an image's pictures go together.
  variant image-pictures {
    /// One picture.
    still,
    /// Frames shown one after another, as a GIF's, an animated PNG's, WebP's or a HEIF sequence's.
    animation(image-animation),
    /// Pictures each of its own, as a TIFF's pages or the images of a HEIF collection.
    pages(image-pages),
  }

  /// An animation: its frames, and how many times it plays through them.
  record image-animation {
    /// Its frames, in order; two at least.
    frames: list<animation-frame>,
    /// How many times it plays through its frames.
    plays: animation-plays,
  }

  /// One frame of an animation.
  record animation-frame {
    /// How long it shows; none where the file says nothing, for which a program picks its own.
    duration: option<duration>,
  }

  /// How many times an animation plays through its frames.
  variant animation-plays {
    /// This many times, one at least.
    times(u32),
    forever,
  }

  /// A document's pages, and which of them the file names as its main picture.
  record image-pages {
    /// Its pages, in order; two at least.
    pages: list<image-page>,
    /// The index of the page the file names as its main picture: its first, except where a
    /// HEIF names another.
    primary: u32,
  }

  /// One page of a document. Each page has its own size and orientation.
  record image-page {
    /// Its size in pixels, upright.
    size: size,
    orientation: image-orientation,
  }

  /// How the picture a file stores is turned from upright: EXIF's `Orientation`, mirrored left to
  /// right where `mirrored`, then turned clockwise by `rotation`, to show it upright.
  record image-orientation {
    mirrored: bool,
    rotation: image-rotation,
  }

  /// How far a picture is turned clockwise.
  enum image-rotation {
    none,
    quarter-turn,
    half-turn,
    three-quarter-turn,
  }

  /// What an image's file says of how it was taken: EXIF's fields a photo app shows. A field is
  /// none where the file says nothing.
  record image-metadata {
    /// When it was taken, as the camera's clock read it: EXIF's `DateTimeOriginal`, with its
    /// `OffsetTimeOriginal`.
    taken: option<date-time>,
    /// The camera's maker and model, as the camera names itself: EXIF's `Make` and `Model`.
    camera-make: option<string>,
    camera-model: option<string>,
    /// The lens, as the camera names it: EXIF's `LensModel`.
    lens-model: option<string>,
    /// How long the shutter was open, in seconds: EXIF's `ExposureTime`.
    exposure-time: option<f64>,
    /// The aperture, as an f-number: EXIF's `FNumber`.
    f-number: option<f64>,
    /// The sensor's sensitivity, as an ISO speed: EXIF's `PhotographicSensitivity`.
    iso: option<u32>,
    /// The lens's focal length, in millimetres: EXIF's `FocalLength`.
    focal-length: option<f64>,
    /// The focal length on a 35 mm film camera with the same view, in millimetres: EXIF's
    /// `FocalLengthIn35mmFilm`.
    equivalent-focal-length: option<f64>,
    /// How far the exposure was set from the camera's metering, in stops: EXIF's
    /// `ExposureBiasValue`.
    exposure-bias: option<f64>,
    /// Whether the flash fired: EXIF's `Flash`.
    flash-fired: option<bool>,
    /// Where it was taken: EXIF's GPS position.
    location: option<location>,
  }

  /// A date and time of day as a clock read it, and how far that clock was from UTC.
  record date-time {
    year: s32,
    /// From 1, January, to 12.
    month: u8,
    /// From 1.
    day: u8,
    hour: u8,
    minute: u8,
    second: u8,
    /// Minutes ahead of UTC, negative behind it; none where the file does not say, so the moment
    /// is the camera's local time, wherever it was.
    utc-offset: option<s16>,
  }

  /// A place on Earth, as GPS gives it: WGS 84.
  record location {
    /// Degrees north of the equator, negative south of it.
    latitude: f64,
    /// Degrees east of Greenwich, negative west of it.
    longitude: f64,
    /// Metres above sea level, negative below it.
    altitude: option<f64>,
  }
}
